# Playwright conventions

## 1. Locators

Resolve elements in this order only: `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` or `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId` (escape hatch — comment why nothing higher worked). Narrow a multi-match with `.filter({ hasText })`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — pages/ProgramsPage.ts
this.heading = page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Programs', level: 2 });

programRow(name: string): Locator {
  return this.table.getByRole('row').filter({ hasText: name });
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
page.locator('table tbody tr').first();
page.locator('//button[contains(@class,"edit")]');
```

**Why:** A CSS or XPath locator, or `.first()`, follows DOM order, so an extra row or restyled button makes the test act on the wrong element.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with a web-first assertion that retries: `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `.toBeEnabled()`, or `.toHaveText()`. Never `waitForTimeout`. Never snapshot a boolean.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — tests/ds1-create-new-academic-program.spec.ts
await expect(programsPage.programEditButton(programName)).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** A fixed sleep races the app, and a one-shot `isVisible()` fails when the element appears a moment later.

## 3. Page objects

One class per page or component in `pages/`. The spec states intent and holds every `expect`. The page object holds `readonly` locators and action methods (`goto`, `clickX`, `fillY`, `submit`) and does not assert. Compose a child object for a distinct component. Instantiate with `new XxxPage(page)`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — pages/ProgramsPage.ts
export class ProgramsPage {
  readonly newProgramModal: NewProgramModal;

  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
    this.newProgramButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: '+ New Program' });
    this.newProgramModal = new NewProgramModal(page);
  }

  async openNewProgramForm(): Promise<void> {
    await this.newProgramButton.click();
  }
}

// spec
const programsPage = new ProgramsPage(page);
await programsPage.openNewProgramForm();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramModal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — locator and assertion inside the spec, expect inside the page object
await page.getByRole('button', { name: '+ New Program' }).click();

async openNewProgramForm(): Promise<void> {
  await expect(this.newProgramButton).toBeVisible();
  await this.newProgramButton.click();
}
```

**Why:** An inline locator is copied into every spec, so one label change breaks the suite, and an `expect` inside the page object hides which behavior the test meant to check.

## 4. Auth

Stored session is the default. The setup project runs `tests/auth.setup.ts` once, signs in through the UI, and writes `playwright/.auth/user.json`. The `chromium` project loads that `storageState`. Login-page tests start empty with `test.use({ storageState: unauthenticatedStorageState })` and sign in through the UI. API login (POST credentials, inject the token) is only for bulk setup, one-off scripts, or a stale storageState.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — login-page test only; fixtures/auth.helpers.ts
test.use({ storageState: unauthenticatedStorageState });

await page.goto('/login');
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(email);
await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(password);
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — UI login in a programs spec that already has storageState
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.DIDAXIS_EMAIL!);
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.DIDAXIS_PASSWORD!);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
});
```

**Why:** Signing in through the UI on every feature test makes an auth flake fail an unrelated scenario and slows the suite.

## 5. Test data

### Layout

| Location | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `test-data/factories/` | Faker-backed factories for happy-path variable input |
| `test-data/invalid/` | Curated invalid or boundary inputs as static `.ts` exports |
| `test-data/enums/` | Repeated API paths, UI routes, and user-visible copy |
| `process.env` | Secrets, `DIDAXIS_URL`, and API bearer tokens only |

```typescript
// ✅ Good — test-data/factories/program.factory.ts
import { buildProgramCreateInput } from '../test-data/factories/program.factory';

const { name, description } = buildProgramCreateInput({ name: 'Data Science Fundamentals' });
await programsPage.createProgram({ name, description });
```

```typescript
// ✅ Good — test-data/invalid/program-names.ts
import { WHITESPACE_ONLY_PROGRAM_NAMES } from '../test-data/invalid/program-names';

for (const name of WHITESPACE_ONLY_PROGRAM_NAMES) {
  await modal.fillProgramName(name);
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ Good — test-data/enums/programs.ts
import { ProgramsApiPath, ProgramsUiPath } from '../test-data/enums/programs';

await request.get(`${process.env.DIDAXIS_URL}${ProgramsApiPath.collection}`, { ... });
await page.goto(ProgramsUiPath.list);
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — inline secret or duplicated path literals across specs
const token = 'eyJhbGciOi...';
await request.get(`${process.env.DIDAXIS_URL}/api/programs`);
```

**Why:** Factories keep happy-path data unique without copy-pasting `Date.now()` helpers; static invalid sets document the cases validation specs must cover; enums stop path drift; env vars keep secrets out of git.

### Cleanup and tracking

Import `{ test, expect }` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture`. When a test creates a program, read `data.id` from `POST /api/programs` and call `trackProgram` immediately. Give each program a unique name (factory or suffix). A `page.route` fulfill does not persist a row — do not track it, and leave the fixture's `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>` unmocked.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — fixtures/program-create.helpers.ts
const body = await response.json();
const id = body?.data?.id;
expect(typeof id, 'Create program response did not include data.id').toBe('string');
trackProgram(id);
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test.afterAll(async ({ request }) => {
  await request.delete(`${process.env.DIDAXIS_URL}/api/programs/${hardcodedId}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer eyJhbGciOi...' },
  });
});
```

**Why:** Rows the test created and never deleted make the next list or duplicate-name test fail, and a hardcoded token expires or lands in git.

## 6. API testing

Call the API with Playwright's `request` fixture. Assert status and the JSON shape. Build the URL from `process.env.DIDAXIS_URL` and send `Authorization: Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` (or `DIDAXIS_API_KEY` when that is unset). Keep UI behavior in a page-object spec.

`tests/api.spec.ts` is empty, and the only `request` calls today are cleanup and the manual purge. Advise this spec:

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised; not in the repo yet
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('GET /api/programs returns a data array', async ({ request }) => {
  const response = await request.get(`${process.env.DIDAXIS_URL}/api/programs`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` },
  });

  await expect(response).toBeOK();
  const body = await response.json();
  expect(Array.isArray(body.data)).toBe(true);
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const body = await page.evaluate(() => fetch('/api/programs').then((res) => res.json()));
expect(body).toBeTruthy();
```

**Why:** `fetch` inside the page drops the status code, so a 401 or 500 looks like a missing button instead of a failed API call.

## 7. Network mocking

Use `page.route` only when the stimulus is a synthetic programs response and the assertion is about the UI. Match pathname `/api/programs` or `/api/programs/:id` and the one method under test, fulfill that call with `contentType: 'application/json'`, and `route.continue()` for every other request. Never mock the endpoint the test is verifying (create, edit, delete, duplicate rejection, list filtering, cleanup). Read the error or empty copy from the live page before asserting it. If the screen renders nothing, assert the Programs heading stays visible and `pageerror` stays empty. One tag per test (usually `@regression` for mocked API/UI edge cases): `{ tag: '@regression' }`.

No spec follows this yet. `tests/ds5-program-list-filtering-and-display.spec.ts` intercepts `**/*`. Advise this route:

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised; not in the repo yet
await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() !== 'POST') {
    await route.continue();
    return;
  }
  await route.fulfill({
    status: 503,
    contentType: 'application/json',
    body: JSON.stringify({ message: 'Service unavailable' }),
  });
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.route('**/*', async (route) => {
  await route.fulfill({ status: 500, body: 'nope' });
});

// also bad: mocking POST /api/programs inside a test that claims to verify create
```

**Why:** A catch-all route swallows auth refresh and assets, so the test fails on a blank shell, and mocking the call under test hides whether create or delete actually works.

### Programs API edge cases

Required: save `POST /api/programs` and save `PUT` or `PATCH /api/programs/:id` fulfill **503** and assert the error copy you read; list `GET /api/programs` fulfill **200** `[]` and assert the empty copy you read; list `GET` fulfill **200** with a body that is not a program list (`{}`, `null`, `"ok"`, `{ "data": null }`) and assert the app does not crash.

Also cover, after reading the screen: list **404**, **500**, **501**, **502**, **503** (error copy when the UI shows one); list **401** (message or redirect to `/login`); list **403** (access-denied outcome); list **204** and **300** (no crash); list **200** with `Content-Type: text/html` (no crash). Save **400**, **401**, **403**, **422**, **500**, **501**, **502**; **404** on `PUT` or `PATCH` only; **200** whose body has no program object (UI-only — keep it out of a real create or edit test); **409** as its own UI test (the duplicate-name spec keeps the live API). Delete **404**, **500**, **501**, **502**, **503**. A successful delete uses the live `DELETE`.

## 8. Accessibility

Scan with `new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).analyze()` and `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])`. Scope with `.include()` or `.exclude()` only for a noisy third-party widget, and comment why. Add the keyboard path axe cannot do: tab to the primary control, `toBeFocused()`, Enter, dialog open. Drive both through the existing POMs. One tag per test. If the scan finds real violations, report them and stop.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts (assert the violations array directly)
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);

await programsPage.tabToNewProgramButton();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramButton).toBeFocused();
await page.keyboard.press('Enter');
await expect(programsPage.newProgramModal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).disableRules(['color-contrast']).analyze();
expect(results.violations.length).toBeLessThan(5);
```

**Why:** `.disableRules()` or a length threshold turns a real WCAG failure into a green run, so the defect never gets reported.

## 9. Assertions

Keep every `expect` in the spec. Use auto-retrying matchers (`toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`, `toContainText`, `toHaveURL`, `toEqual` on the axe violations array). One tag per test. When hardening a spec, change locators and waits only — do not change what the assertion checks. Assert the copy the app renders; do not invent it.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await expect(programsPage.programRow(programName)).toContainText(programName);
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/login/);
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const text = await programsPage.programRow(programName).innerText();
expect(text.includes('program') || text.length > 0).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** A one-shot `innerText()` does not retry, and a loosened expected string passes while the product shows the wrong value.

## 10. Anti-flake

`playwright.config.ts` owns runner stability: `fullyParallel: true`, `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0` (never above 2, never retry locally), `baseURL` from `process.env.DIDAXIS_URL`, `trace: 'on-first-retry'`, `locale: 'en-US'`, `timezoneId: 'America/New_York'`, and the setup project plus `storageState` dependency. Do not set `workers: 1`. Do not edit that config from a spec, POM, or fixture.

Frozen time: when the assertion is a relative timestamp (`just now`, `2 minutes ago`), install the clock in the spec before navigation or any page script reads `Date`. Keep the expected label.

No spec installs the clock yet. Advise this:

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised; install in the spec, then assert the frozen label
test('edited program shows a frozen relative timestamp', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-06-15T19:00:00Z') });

  const programsPage = new ProgramsPage(page);
  await programsPage.gotoAndWaitForTable();

  await expect(programsPage.programRow(programName)).toContainText('2 minutes ago');
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test.describe.configure({ retries: 5 });
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
await expect(row).toContainText(`${minutesAgo(new Date())} minutes ago`);
```

**Why:** A label derived from the wall clock changes while the test runs, and extra retries or `workers: 1` hide that race instead of fixing it.

## 11. Tags

Assign **exactly one** Playwright tag per `test()` — never on `describe`. Allowed values:

| Tag | When to use |
|-----|-------------|
| `@smoke` | Critical path after deploy; smallest set that proves the app is usable |
| `@sanity` | Quick breadth check; more than smoke, still fast |
| `@regression` | Ticket coverage, edge cases, a11y, and mocked API/UI scenarios |
| `@api` | Contract or HTTP checks via the `request` fixture (no full UI journey) |
| `@e2e` | Full authenticated UI flows through POMs (default for story specs) |
| `@destructive` | **Only** when the test mutates **shared or global** state (locale, roles, feature flags, tenant settings) |

A test that creates data **it** deletes (for example `trackProgram` and the cleanup fixture) keeps its importance tag (`@smoke`, `@regression`, `@e2e`, …) — **not** `@destructive`.

Run slices with npm scripts (`package.json`): `test:smoke`, `test:sanity`, `test:regression`, `test:api`, `test:e2e`, and `test:destructive` (last runs `playwright test --grep @destructive --workers=1`). Do not set `workers: 1` in `playwright.config.ts` for the whole suite.

Any `@destructive` test **must** register `test.afterEach` and/or `test.afterAll` that **reverts** the shared mutation (restore prior locale, role, flag, or setting), even when the test body fails.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — importance tag; self-cleaned data
test('creates a program', { tag: '@e2e' }, async ({ page, trackProgram }) => {
  const { name, description } = buildProgramCreateInput();
  await programsPage.createProgram({ name, description });
  // trackProgram in create helper — fixture deletes after the test
});

// ✅ Good — shared state; serial slice; explicit revert
test('switches org locale to fr-CA', { tag: '@destructive' }, async ({ page, request }) => {
  const priorLocale = await readOrgLocale(request);
  try {
    await setOrgLocaleViaUi(page, 'fr-CA');
    await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Programmes' })).toBeVisible();
  } finally {
    await restoreOrgLocale(request, priorLocale);
  }
});

test.afterEach(async ({ request }) => {
  await restoreOrgLocale(request, defaultLocale);
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — tag on describe, or two tags, or @destructive for tracked create/delete
test.describe('Programs', { tag: '@smoke' }, () => { /* ... */ });

test('...', { tag: ['@smoke', '@e2e'] }, async () => { /* ... */ });

test('creates program', { tag: '@destructive' }, async ({ trackProgram }) => {
  /* only mutates data this test deletes */
});
```

**Why:** `--grep` slices power CI tiers; `@destructive` plus `--workers=1` avoids parallel tests clobbering shared settings; revert hooks keep the next run from starting in a bad global state.

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|----|--------|
| `getByRole`, then label, text, test id; `.filter({ hasText })` | CSS, XPath, or `.first()` |
| `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `.toHaveText()` | `waitForTimeout`, or `expect(await locator.isVisible())` |
| Actions on a POM; every `expect` in the spec | Inline locators in the spec, or `expect` inside `pages/` |
| `storageState` from `auth.setup.ts`; empty state only for login tests | UI login in every feature spec |
| `trackProgram(data.id)` from the create response; factories under `test-data/factories/` | `afterAll` cleanup, a hardcoded token, or tracking a mocked id |
| One of `@smoke` … `@destructive` on each `test()`; revert hook on `@destructive` | Tag on `describe`, multiple tags, or `@destructive` for self-deleted rows |
| `request.get` / `request.delete` with the env bearer and `DIDAXIS_URL` | `page.evaluate(() => fetch(...))` as the API check |
| `page.route` for one method and pathname; `route.continue()` the rest | `page.route('**/*')`, or mocking the endpoint the test verifies |
| Axe `wcag2a` + `wcag2aa`, then keyboard focus and Enter | `.disableRules()` to go green |
| Web-first matchers; one tag; same assertion when hardening | A one-shot `innerText()` check, or a weaker expected string |
| `page.clock.install({ time })` before navigation; CI retries `2`, local `0` | `workers: 1`, retries above 2, or a timestamp read from `new Date()` |

## Refusals (hard stops)

Stop and raise the conflict. Do not comply, work around, or soften these:

- **Weaken or delete an assertion to go green** — refuse. Keep the assertion; fix the locator/wait or report the product bug.
- **Heal a failure triage classified as a real bug** — refuse. Hand it to a human / bug-reporter; do not change the test to match broken behavior.
- **Replace a role-based locator with CSS or XPath** — refuse. Keep `getByRole` (then label / text / test id); escalate if nothing higher works.
- **Use `waitForTimeout`** — refuse. Use a web-first assertion that retries.
- **Hardcode a credential or token** — refuse. Read secrets from env / `storageState` only.
- **Use the `any` type** — refuse. Type the value or use a narrow unknown check.
